---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { SITE_URL } from "@/lib/seo";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildArticleSchema } from "@/lib/schema";

const title = "Base64 Encoding Explained — Complete Guide | ToolBite";
const description = "What Base64 is, why it exists, common use cases (data URIs, JWT, email), Base64 vs Base64url, and why it is NOT encryption.";
const canonicalPath = "/guides/base64-encoding-guide.html";
const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}${canonicalPath}`;
const schemas = [
  buildArticleSchema({ title, description, url: pageUrl }),
  buildBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: "Home", url: SITE_URL },
    { name: "Guides", url: `${SITE_URL}/guides.html` },
    { name: "Base64 Encoding Guide", url: pageUrl }
  ])
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  schemas={schemas}
  badge="Developer"
  badgeColor="#4A9FE0"
  icon="📎"
  readTime="4 min read"
>
  <article class="gl-article">

    <div class="gl-box gl-box--info">
      <span class="gl-box-icon">💡</span>
      <span><strong>Best for:</strong> developers working with APIs, email systems, JWT tokens, or embedding images and files directly in HTML and CSS.</span>
    </div>

    <h2>What is Base64?</h2>
    <p>Base64 is an <strong>encoding scheme</strong> that converts binary data into a string using 64 printable ASCII characters (A–Z, a–z, 0–9, +, /). It was designed to safely transmit binary data over text-based systems like SMTP email and HTTP headers that weren't built to handle raw bytes.</p>
    <p>The name comes from the 64 characters used. Each character encodes 6 bits (2⁶ = 64). The output is always about <strong>33% larger</strong> than the original.</p>

    <h2>How encoding works</h2>
    <ol class="gl-steps">
      <li>Take the binary input (bytes of an image, string, or file).</li>
      <li>Group into 3-byte chunks (24 bits each).</li>
      <li>Split each 24-bit chunk into four 6-bit groups.</li>
      <li>Map each 6-bit group to one of the 64 Base64 characters.</li>
      <li>Pad with <code>=</code> characters if the input length isn't divisible by 3.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Example: the string <code>Hi!</code> encodes to <code>SGkh</code>. You can verify this in <a href="/tools/base64-encoder.html">ToolBite's Base64 encoder</a>.</p>

    <h2>Common use cases</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>API authentication</strong> — Basic Auth sends <code>username:password</code> encoded in Base64 in the Authorization header.</li>
      <li><strong>JWT tokens</strong> — Header and payload sections are Base64url-encoded (a URL-safe variant).</li>
      <li><strong>Data URIs</strong> — Embedding small images directly in HTML: <code>src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw..."</code></li>
      <li><strong>Email attachments</strong> — MIME uses Base64 to encode binary attachments for SMTP transit.</li>
      <li><strong>Binary in JSON</strong> — JSON is text-only; binary data (files, images, keys) is Base64-encoded before storing in JSON fields.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Base64 is NOT encryption</h2>
    <div class="gl-box gl-box--warn">
      <span class="gl-box-icon">⚠️</span>
      <span><strong>Base64 is reversible by anyone, instantly, with no key required.</strong> It provides zero security. Never use it to "hide" passwords, API secrets, or sensitive data.</span>
    </div>
    <p>Seeing a Base64 string doesn't mean the data is encrypted — it's just encoded for safe transport. Anyone can decode it in one second. For actual security use proper encryption (AES-256) or hashing (SHA-256, bcrypt for passwords).</p>

    <h2>Base64 vs Base64url</h2>
    <div class="gl-quick-ref">
      <h3>Comparison</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Base64</strong> uses <code>+</code> and <code>/</code> — these are special in URLs and need percent-encoding.</li>
        <li><strong>Base64url</strong> replaces <code>+</code> with <code>-</code> and <code>/</code> with <code>_</code> — safe in URLs and HTTP headers without escaping.</li>
        <li>JWT tokens use Base64url. Data URIs use standard Base64.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>JavaScript quick reference</h2>
    <ul class="gl-checklist">
      <li>Encode: <code>btoa(str)</code> — works for ASCII strings only.</li>
      <li>Decode: <code>atob(str)</code> — returns the original string.</li>
      <li>For Unicode: <code>btoa(unescape(encodeURIComponent(str)))</code>.</li>
      <li>For files/binary: use <code>FileReader.readAsDataURL()</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Quick reference facts</h2>
    <ul class="gl-checklist">
      <li>Base64 output is ~33% larger than input.</li>
      <li>Strings end with 0, 1, or 2 padding <code>=</code> characters.</li>
      <li>Base64 ≠ encryption — never use it for security.</li>
      <li>JWT tokens use Base64url (not standard Base64).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="gl-cta-row">
      <a href="/tools/base64-encoder.html" class="tb-btn tb-btn-primary">Open Base64 Encoder →</a>
      <a href="/guides/jwt-decoder-guide.html" class="tb-btn tb-btn-outline">JWT guide</a>
      <a href="/guides/hash-functions-explained.html" class="tb-btn tb-btn-outline">Hash functions guide</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section>
    <p class="gl-section-heading">Tools used in this guide</p>
    <div class="gl-tools-row">
      <a href="/tools/base64-encoder.html" class="gl-tool-btn"><span aria-hidden="true">📎</span> Base64 Encoder</a>
      <a href="/tools/url-encoder.html" class="gl-tool-btn"><span aria-hidden="true">🌐</span> URL Encoder</a>
      <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder.html" class="gl-tool-btn"><span aria-hidden="true">🪪</span> JWT Decoder</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="gl-section-heading">Related guides & tools</p>
    <div class="gl-related">
      <a href="/guides/jwt-decoder-guide.html" class="gl-card">
        <span class="gl-card-icon">🪪</span>
        <p class="gl-card-title">JWT Decoder Guide</p>
        <p class="gl-card-desc">Base64url in action — how JWT tokens use encoding.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/guides/hash-functions-explained.html" class="gl-card">
        <span class="gl-card-icon">🔢</span>
        <p class="gl-card-title">Hash Functions</p>
        <p class="gl-card-desc">SHA-256 and bcrypt — the actual security Base64 isn't.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/guides/url-encoding-guide.html" class="gl-card">
        <span class="gl-card-icon">🌐</span>
        <p class="gl-card-title">URL Encoding Guide</p>
        <p class="gl-card-desc">The other encoding you'll encounter in every URL.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
